#
# The kernel the entry image boots: Apple's RELEASE kernel plus the one filesystem that can be root
# on a device with no storage driver, plus the one attribute that lets a process be exec'd at all.
#
# Experiment 459 needs two things of this configuration and nothing else, and both are *words* in
# a configuration file rather than code:
#
#   mockfs    - `bsd/miscfs/mockfs/` is the only filesystem in the tree whose `vfc_mountroot` is
#               non-NULL (`bsd/vfs/vfs_conf.c`'s `vfstbllist`; devfs and routefs have neither a
#               mountroot nor VFC_VFSCANMOUNTROOT, and NFSCLIENT is off in this configuration).
#               `vfs_mountroot` therefore skips every other entry and mounts mockfs over whatever
#               `rootdev` names - which is how a kernel with no HFS, no BlockStorage and no eMMC
#               driver gets a root filesystem at all. Apple's own comment for the option in
#               `config/MASTER:123` says what it is for: "Boot from an executable".
#
# Experiment 468 adds the second word, and its reason is a wall 467's run measured rather than a
# preference. With process 1's exec reaching the image activators for the first time, the file it
# reads is claimed by none of them (`ENOEXEC`, `kern_exec.c:2803-2805`) - and the obvious next step,
# a real Mach-O on the RAM disk, cannot be loaded by *any* configuration of `parse_machfile` in
# this tree:
#
#   - a **static** executable (`MH_EXECUTE` without `MH_DYLDLINK`) is refused outright at
#     `bsd/kern/mach_loader.c:631-636`, whose comment is the answer: "Check properties of static
#     executables (disallowed except for development)" - `#if !(DEVELOPMENT || DEBUG) return
#     (LOAD_FAILURE)`. A static binary is legal in exactly the two configurations the comment
#     names.
#   - a **dynamic** one (`MH_DYLDLINK`) needs both a dylinker vnode (`:1155`, and mockfs has one
#     regular file) and `CS_DYLD_PLATFORM` in its code-signing flags (`:1173`), which is set only
#     for a *platform binary* (`:2326-2327`) - i.e. it needs Apple's signature.
#
# So this configuration takes the branch Apple's own comment names. `development` - the attribute
# `config/MASTER:611`'s `options DEVELOPMENT # dev kernel # <development>` is tagged with - is
# spelled in lower case *because that is how the tag names a configuration*, and it is the narrow
# spelling: measured, it differs from `[ RELEASE mockfs ]` by exactly two `-D` flags -
# `-DDEVELOPMENT=1` added, and `CONFIG_MSG_BSIZE` switched from `CONFIG_MSG_BSIZE_REL` to
# `CONFIG_MSG_BSIZE_DEV`, whose values `config/MASTER:288-289` both give as 4096. `DEVELOPMENT`
# (upper case) is a different thing: the *dev attribute list* (`KERNEL_DEV BSD_DEV FILESYS_DEV
# NFS SKYWALK_DEV ...`, `MASTER.arm:66`), which adds twenty-two more macros (`MACH_ASSERT`,
# `CONFIG_DTRACE`, `CONFIG_ZLEAKS`, `CONFIG_VNGUARD`, `INTERRUPT_MASKED_DEBUG`, ...) and is not
# this step.
#
# **And the value has to come from here rather than from the command line.** Every component's
# `meta_features.h` includes `development.h`, which `tools/gen_option_headers.py` writes as
# `#define DEVELOPMENT 0` or `1` from *this* configuration; a `-DDEVELOPMENT=1` on the compile line
# is overridden by it, so with the flag alone `#ifdef DEVELOPMENT` is true while `#if DEVELOPMENT`
# is false -- the "an off option has two spellings" class this project has recorded, measured here
# as a `.data` change in `mach_loader.o` with a byte-identical `.text`.
#
# This is `RELEASE` by *composition*, not by copy: a configuration's attributes may themselves be
# configuration names (tools/xnu_config/expand.sh), so `[ RELEASE mockfs development ]` reuses
# MASTER.arm's own RELEASE attribute list rather than restating it, and two options are added to
# it. A second copy of that list here would be the "one value, two definitions" defect this project
# has recorded twenty times.
#
#  STAGE90_XNU = [ RELEASE mockfs development ]
